  "geodesic_conventions_inspected": {
    "geometry": "Oriented finite-type hyperbolic curvature -1, no cone singularities. Each theorem adds its own compactness, cusp and boundary hypotheses.",
    "curves": "Unoriented free-homotopy classes by default. Figure-eights are primitive one-transverse-double-point classes; the torus contributes two N labels and one H label per simple alpha. Equal lengths do not identify labels.",
    "traces": "Positive geometric t=2cosh(ell/2), cusp t=2. Pants lifts trA=t_a, trB=t_b, trAB=-t_c; cusp parabolic factorization instead trA=t_a,trB=-t_b,AB=P. These are local compatible signs, not a universal positive SL(2) lift. Section 23 retains signed characters.",
    "twists": "Signed hyperbolic displacement with Dehn-twist period a>0. Angles and endpoint tangential coordinates use the compatible signed convention. Fixed curve length is not periodic; its orbit sum is.",
    "endpoint_phase": "Orient by travel, p in (-1,1); dL=-p_s ds+p_t dt. Common cuff coordinate after reversal of one induced boundary orientation; opposite-side crossing plus equal p matches the full unit tangent.",
    "branches": "acosh nonnegative real branch on [1,infinity); all displayed square roots nonnegative; invalid domains are not clamped.",
    "cusps": "lambda=exp(signed truncated distance/2), without sqrt(2). H denotes an embedded sufficiently small horocycle for literal truncation, or a positive decoration with signed length when larger. Gap partition assumes no geodesic boundary.",
    "basmajian": "Oriented orthogeodesics contribute 2log(coth(d/2)); grouping reverse orientations gives 4 for one unoriented arc. The boundary spectrum includes nonsimple arcs.",
    "WP": "Same orbifold measure on both unfolded quotients. Punctured-torus stack measure includes one ineffective factor 1/2, consistent with reviewed Section 6.",
    "numerics": "Author finite checks use exact SymPy identities and mpmath at 70/110 digits. Floating residuals are not intervals. Analytic truncation bounds are separate from quadrature/roundoff; tiny positive signals use stable or scaled forms."
  },
